---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import HashGenerator from '../../components/HashGenerator.jsx';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="Hash Generator (MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-512)"
  description="Generate MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, and SHA-512 hashes from text instantly, entirely in your browser."
>
  <HashGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>What hashing is for</h2>
  <p>
    Hash functions turn input text into a fixed-length string of characters
    — the same input always produces the same hash, but you can't reverse
    the hash back to the original input. Common uses include checksums for
    file integrity, cache keys, and (with proper algorithms) password
    storage.
  </p>

  <h2>Which algorithm should I use?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>MD5</strong> — fast, but broken for security purposes. Fine for checksums, not for anything security-sensitive.</li>
    <li><strong>SHA-1</strong> — also considered broken for cryptographic security, still used in some legacy systems (e.g. git commit hashes).</li>
    <li><strong>SHA-256 / SHA-512</strong> — current standard for integrity checks and non-password security uses.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Hash Generator FAQ</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Should I use this for password hashing?</strong> No — for
    passwords, use a purpose-built algorithm like bcrypt, scrypt, or
    argon2 on your server, which are designed to be slow and resistant to
    brute-force attacks. General-purpose hashes like SHA-256 are too fast
    for safe password storage.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Is my input sent anywhere?</strong> No — all hashing happens
    locally in your browser using the Web Crypto API and a client-side MD5
    implementation.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>
